## 1. Historical collision changed the target

CW-002C proposed using observation-dependent subrectangular words as the next extension beyond primitive transition matrices.

A dedicated collision search found that this contraction mechanism has already been developed much further than the previous lab note reflected.

### Kaijser 1975

For a finite HMM with stepping matrices \(M(y)\), Kaijser's Condition A requires a finite observation word

\[
w=(y_1,\ldots,y_\ell)
\]

whose product

\[
A_w=M(y_1)\cdots M(y_\ell)
\]

is nonzero and subrectangular.

Kaijser used this to prove filter convergence/unique ergodicity under the associated Markov assumptions.

### Kochman–Reeds 2006

They gave a simpler and slightly stronger proof of Kaijser's unique-ergodicity result.

### Chigansky–van Handel 2010

They later gave necessary and sufficient conditions for Blackwell's unique-ergodicity problem and showed, for finite state/observation spaces, that the rank-one closure condition is the correct qualitative criterion.

### Shuval–Tal 2018/2024

This is the most direct collision with CW-002C.

They explicitly define the Birkhoff coefficient for nonzero subrectangular matrices, prove that repeated subrectangular factors separated by arbitrary nonnegative factors contract projective information multiplicatively, count nonoverlapping contracting occurrences, and use the result to bound conditional mutual information.

Therefore the generic theorem

\[
\text{subrectangular-word occurrences}
\Rightarrow
\text{multiplicative projective forgetting}
\]

is classical/prior art.

The research target is narrowed to:

\[
\boxed{
\text{projective word forgetting}
\to
\text{conditional future-tail experiment}
\to
\text{Le Cam null deficiency}
\to
\text{loss-universal stopping}.
}
\]

