# 8. Rank-one word = exact synchronization

If

\[
\tau_w=0,
\]

then the active submatrix of \(A_w\) has rank one projectively.

After a single occurrence,

\[
\pi_n^{x,h}
=
\pi_n^{x',h}
\]

for every surviving pair \(x,x'\).

Hence

\[
\boxed{
r(E_{n,h})=0.
}
\]

The future can no longer distinguish any surviving initial hypotheses.

All residual information about the initial state is already contained in the observed past/support elimination.

This is an exact statistical synchronization word.

It has a direct conceptual bridge to synchronizing/weighted automata, where rank-one products reset distributions/states.

---

# 9. Full-row contracting words give uniform occurrence probabilities

Now impose the stronger condition

\[
R(A_w)=\mathcal X.
\]

Then the word is possible from every hidden state.

Define

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_w
=
\min_{i\in\mathcal X}
\sum_j(A_w)_{ij}
>0.
}
\]

For any current hidden-state distribution \(\pi\),

\[
P(w\text{ occurs in the next }\ell\text{-block}\mid\pi)
=
\pi A_w\mathbf 1
\ge
\alpha_w.
\]

Thus, on disjoint length-\(\ell\) blocks, the occurrence indicator has conditional success probability at least \(\alpha_w\), regardless of the previous observation history.

Let

\[
N_B
\]

be the number of successful \(w\)-blocks among the first \(B\) disjoint blocks.

Then \(N_B\) stochastically dominates a

\[
\operatorname{Binomial}(B,\alpha_w)
\]

random variable.

Consequently:

\[
\boxed{
P(N_B<q)
\le
\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}
\binom Bj
\alpha_w^j(1-\alpha_w)^{B-j}.
}
\]

No independence of the HMM blocks is required; the uniform conditional minorization is enough.

---

# 10. High-probability decision horizon

For a target residual value \(\varepsilon\), define

\[
q_\varepsilon
=
\min
\left\{
q\ge1:
\left(1-\frac1m\right)
\tanh
\left(
\frac{\Delta_w\tau_w^{q-1}}4
\right)
\le
\varepsilon
\right\}.
\]

Because a full-row word never eliminates an initial hypothesis, \(s=m\).

If

\[
N_B\ge q_\varepsilon,
\]

then the entire future-tail deficiency is at most \(\varepsilon\).

Hence:

### Theorem CW-002D.3 — Binomial-tail horizon

\[
\boxed{
P\left(
r(E_{B\ell,H_{B\ell}})>\varepsilon
\right)
\le
\sum_{j=0}^{q_\varepsilon-1}
\binom Bj
\alpha_w^j(1-\alpha_w)^{B-j}.
}
\]

Also, if \(\tau_{q_\varepsilon}\) is the block index of the \(q_\varepsilon\)-th occurrence,

\[
\boxed{
E[\tau_{q_\varepsilon}]
\le
\frac{q_\varepsilon}{\alpha_w}.
}
\]

Thus Kaijser's qualitative "a contracting word exists" mechanism becomes a finite-time loss-universal decision-memory certificate when a uniform occurrence probability is available.

