# 11. Concrete finite example

Use two stepping matrices

\[
M(a)=
\begin{pmatrix}
.20&.10&0&0\\
.10&.20&0&0\\
.15&.15&0&0\\
.05&.25&0&0
\end{pmatrix}
\]

and \(M(b)\) chosen so that

\[
M(a)+M(b)
\]

is row-stochastic.

The single-letter word \(a\) is full-row subrectangular with active columns \(\{1,2\}\).

Its active projective quantities are

\[
\Delta_a=\log 10\approx2.302585,
\]

\[
\tau_a
=
\tanh(\Delta_a/4)
\approx0.519494.
\]

Its occurrence probability has the uniform lower bound

\[
\alpha_a
=
\min_i\|e_i^\top M(a)\|_1
=
0.3.
\]

For \(m=4\), to certify residual future decision value

\[
\varepsilon=0.01,
\]

seven occurrences suffice:

\[
q_{0.01}=7.
\]

Thirty-seven disjoint blocks give

\[
P(N_{37}<7)
\le
P(\operatorname{Bin}(37,.3)<7)
\approx0.04397.
\]

Hence after 37 blocks, with probability at least about \(95.6\%\), the entire remaining future has at most \(0.01\) normalized Le Cam decision value about the initial state.

This is illustrative, not an optimal bound.

